From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:42:34 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 14/34] Use HACL*'s vec128 ChaCha20 on the PowerPC targets the
 assembly cannot serve

freebl's accelerated ChaCha20 is the hand-written VSX in chacha20-ppc64le.S,
which the dispatcher gates on __LITTLE_ENDIAN__. Big-endian POWER8 and later
therefore fall back to scalar ChaCha20 despite having VSX, and a PPC970
(PowerMac G4/G5) has no accelerated path at all.

Use Hacl_Chacha20_Vec128 on both. It compiles against whichever vec128
backend libintvector.h selects, so the same source covers VSX on big-endian
POWER8+ and plain AltiVec on the 970, and little-endian keeps the assembly.
USE_PPC_CHACHA20_VEC128 in ppc-crypto.h picks between them and honours
NSS_DISABLE_CRYPTO_VSX and NSS_DISABLE_ALTIVEC.

Two gaps had to be closed in libintvector.h. The existing PowerPC backend's
load32_le/store32_le and load64_le/store64_le use vec_xl and vec_xst, which
keep the CPU's byte order, so on big-endian they now reverse the bytes within
each element. And the 970 has neither vec_xl/vec_xst nor a 64-bit vector
element type, so it gets its own backend: unaligned access by the classic
two-load plus vec_lvsl sequence, and byte permutes for the 64-bit interleaves.

chacha20poly1305-ppc.c grows a Chacha20Poly1305_vec128_aead_{encrypt,decrypt}
pair reusing the poly1305_do_32 already there. Poly1305 stays scalar: its
vec128 form needs 64-bit vector adds and a 32x32->64 multiply the 970 lacks,
and it is only about an eighth of the AEAD's cost. Inputs under two blocks
stay scalar, where the vector setup does not pay for itself.

AEAD gain over scalar at a 1440-byte record: 2.1x on big-endian POWER9,
1.8x on 970-targeted code.

# Conflicts:
#	security/nss/lib/freebl/verified/libintvector.h
